narrative:
Traveling with Corwin turns out to mean something specific: up before light, a pace that doesn't vary whether the road is easy or brutal, and a silence that isn't unfriendly so much as fully occupied elsewhere.

@Corwin: Her name's Elsa. I should have led with that. Three winters of saying "my sister" to strangers and I forget people need a name to actually look for someone.

He unrolls the map again, more out of habit now than hope of new information.

@Corwin: We can chase rumors - there's always rumors, half of them nonsense. We can check the waypoint villages methodically, which is slower and has actually found things before. Or we retrace exactly the route she'd have taken, mile for mile, which I've done twice already and will apparently do a third time if you're willing.

The first waypoint village yields nothing new, but the innkeeper remembers a woman asking after river routes north, three winters back, and describes her well enough that Corwin goes very still listening to it.

@Corwin: That's her. That's - that's the first real thing anyone's told me in eight months.

His hands aren't quite steady when he thanks the innkeeper.

The current takes you both off your feet halfway across, and it's a scramble more than a crossing by the time you both drag yourselves out the far side, bruised, gear half-soaked, dignity fully soaked.

@Corwin: Well. We're across. That's - that's the important part.

He says it through chattering teeth, still checking his map is dry before checking anything else.

An old net-mender remembers her clearly enough once you describe her - not because she was memorable exactly, but because she asked careful, specific questions about a town called Millhaven, inland, and because she asked under a name that wasn't her own.

@Net-mender: Called herself Essa, when she bought passage upriver. Didn't correct anyone who assumed it was short for something. People who rename themselves usually have their reasons, and it's usually not my business to ask what.

Corwin goes very still.

@Corwin: She - she changed her name. That's not someone who got lost. That's someone who left on purpose and didn't want to be followed.

