The nets are half-mended and your back aches before the old woman working beside you mentions, almost offhand, a traveler three winters back who asked careful questions about a town called Millhaven, inland - under a name that wasn't her own.

@Net-mender: Called herself Essa. Didn't correct anyone who assumed it was short for something. People who rename themselves usually have their reasons.

Corwin, listening from a few feet away, goes very still.

@Corwin: She changed her name. That's not someone who got lost. That's someone who left on purpose and didn't want to be followed.

narrative:
Whatever you say, her hands go still on the counter, flour and all.

@Elsa: Three years. I suppose that tracks - he'd have looked, wouldn't he. He always did think looking after me was the same thing as actually listening to me.

She doesn't sound angry, exactly. More like someone setting down a weight she's carried so long she'd half-forgotten it was heavy.

@Elsa: He knew about the marriage. He didn't stop it. I needed to be somewhere he couldn't decide things for me anymore, even with the best intentions in the world. Especially with the best intentions in the world, honestly - those are so much harder to argue with.

You tell Corwin only that you found her, that she's well, and that the rest isn't yours to hand him - it has to come from her, on her terms, whenever she's ready to give it. He doesn't argue. For possibly the first time in three winters, he doesn't try to control how this goes.

Three days later, sitting outside the bakery at dawn, she comes to find you both herself. What she and Corwin say to each other isn't something you're owed a part of. But you watch him walk out an hour later looking like a man who's finally set something down that he's been carrying since before you ever met him.

@Corwin: She's not coming back with us. That's - that's fine. That's actually fine. She wanted me to know that. That's more than I had yesterday.

You tell Corwin the trail went cold in Millhaven - a dead end, like all the others, worse luck than usual. He takes it hard, harder than the others somehow, like some part of him had let himself hope this time.

You carry the truth alone from here. It's not a comfortable weight. It's also, you're fairly sure, the right one - Elsa didn't ask to be found, and being found by someone who loves you isn't automatically a kindness, whatever the person doing the finding believes about their own intentions.

@Corwin: Guess we keep looking, then. Or - guess I keep looking. You've done more than enough already.

You don't wait. You walk Corwin straight through the bakery door, and there's no gentle version of what happens next - Elsa's face goes through disbelief, fury, and something like grief in the space of about two seconds, and Corwin, who has rehearsed this moment for three winters, forgets every word of it the instant he actually sees her.

@Elsa: You have got to be joking. THREE YEARS, and you just - you just walk in?

@Corwin: Elsa - I didn't - I just wanted to know you were safe -

@Elsa: You wanted to know I was where YOU could find me. Those aren't the same thing, and you have never once understood that they aren't the same thing.

There's no clean end to a conversation like that one. Eventually the shouting burns itself out into something quieter and sadder, and Elsa asks, finally, for time - not forgiveness, not yet, maybe not ever, just time and the room to decide on her own what happens from here.

Corwin gives it to her. It's possibly the first thing he's given her, in this whole search, that she actually asked for.

@Corwin: That wasn't - that wasn't how I pictured it. I pictured it going a lot of ways. Not that one.
